---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Heading from "../components/Heading.astro";
import CodeTabs from "../components/CodeTabs.astro";
import { PACKAGES, TIER_LABEL, REPO, GITHUB_ORG } from "../consts";
import mcpTools from "../data/mcp-tools.json";
import skillsData from "../data/skills.json";

// Derived, never typed: this page said "six operations" for weeks after
// compare_grammars shipped. mcp-tools.json is generated from the mcp repo;
// skills.json from the skills repo — so the counts, the skill names and the
// install command below cannot drift from what actually ships.
const TOOL_WORDS = ["zero","one","two","three","four","five","six","seven","eight","nine","ten"];
const nTools = TOOL_WORDS[mcpTools.tools.length] ?? String(mcpTools.tools.length);
const nSkills = TOOL_WORDS[skillsData.skills.length] ?? String(skillsData.skills.length);
const hostedUrl = skillsData.mcp["tabnas-hosted"]?.url;
const stdio = skillsData.mcp["tabnas"];
const stdioCommand = Array.isArray(stdio?.command) ? stdio.command.join(" ") : "";
import type { Tier } from "../consts";

const AGENTS_MD = (name: string) => `${REPO(name)}/blob/main/AGENTS.md`;
const ORDER: Tier[] = ["engine", "tooling", "agent", "grammar", "plugin", "cli"];
const groups = ORDER.map((tier) => ({
  tier,
  label: TIER_LABEL[tier],
  items: PACKAGES.filter((p) => p.tier === tier),
})).filter((g) => g.items.length);
---

<Base
  wide
  title="Agents"
  description="How to build a parser with tabnas — and the distributions an agent installs to do it: Agent Skills, an MCP server, and the tabnas CLI, plus the rule table format, the constraints, and how to verify your work."
>
  <article class="ag">
    <Heading level={1} id="building-with-tabnas">Building with tabnas</Heading>
    <p class="lede">
      Written for an agent that has arrived here to build something. It covers
      the distributions you can install — {nSkills} skills, an MCP server, a
      CLI — the grammar format you should emit, the constraints the engine
      imposes, how to check your work, and where the per-package instructions
      live. A human wanting the argument for any of this should read
      <a href="/why">why tabnas</a> instead.
    </p>

    <section>
      <Heading level={2} id="what-you-are-targeting">1 · What you are targeting</Heading>
      <p>
        A tabnas grammar is <strong>data</strong>: a table of rules, each with
        an <code>open</code> and a <code>close</code> phase, each phase holding
        a list of <em>alternates</em>. An alternate matches a short token
        pattern and may run an action, push a child rule, or repeat the current
        one. Parsing is a for-loop over tokens with a rule stack — no recursion
        and no backtracking.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is the whole machine. You are not writing a parser; you are filling
        in a table. It is deliberately dull, which is what makes it a target you
        can hit reliably.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <Heading level={2} id="skills-and-mcp">2 · Install the workflow: skills, MCP, CLI</Heading>
      <p>
        Everything the rest of this page teaches is also packaged for you to
        install rather than read. Three surfaces, one shared implementation:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong><a href="/skills">The skills</a></strong> — {nSkills}
          portable <a href="https://agentskills.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Agent
          Skills</a> that carry the working procedures:
          {skillsData.skills.map((s, i) => (
            <>
              <a href={`/skills/${s.name}`}><code>{s.name}</code></a>{i < skillsData.skills.length - 1 ? ", " : ". "}
            </>
          ))}
          Each has its own page here, and each is plain markdown a platform
          without plugin support can read straight from
          <a href={REPO("skills")} target="_blank" rel="noopener">the repository</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong><a href="/mcp">The MCP server</a></strong> —
          <code>@tabnas/mcp</code> makes the commands the skills teach
          executable: {nTools} tools to parse, validate a grammar before
          running it, explain a failure, run fixtures, read the plugin
          catalogue, and compare a grammar change against the one it replaces.
          It is <code>dev.tabnas/mcp</code> in the
          <a href="https://registry.modelcontextprotocol.io/v0/servers?search=tabnas" target="_blank" rel="noopener">official
          MCP Registry</a>, so a client that resolves servers from the
          registry finds both transports declared there.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The <code>tabnas</code> CLI</strong> — the same package,
          the same {nTools} operations in a shell. <code>--json</code> prints
          byte-for-byte what the MCP tool returns (golden tests hold the two
          front-ends to it), so a shell transcript and an agent transcript
          describe the same run.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <Heading level={3} id="install-claude-code">In Claude Code</Heading>
      <p>
        One plugin delivers all of it — the {nSkills} skills plus both MCP
        server entries. Two commands, and the second is not optional: adding a
        marketplace registers a catalogue and installs nothing.
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`/plugin marketplace add tabnas/skills\n/plugin install tabnas@tabnas`}</code></pre>

      <Heading level={3} id="install-any-mcp-client">In any MCP client</Heading>
      <p>
        Add the stdio server to your client's MCP configuration — it needs no
        install step of its own; <code>npx</code> fetches it on first run:
      </p>
      <pre><code>{stdioCommand}</code></pre>
      <p>
        If your client cannot spawn a process, the same {nTools} tools are
        served over streamable HTTP at <code>{hostedUrl}</code> — bounded by a
        body cap and a per-IP rate limit, both reported by its
        <code>/.well-known/mcp</code>. Local stdio is the recommended path:
        free, private, unlimited. <a href="/mcp">MCP</a> has the manifest
        JSON, each tool's contract, and the hosted endpoint's bounds.
      </p>

      <Heading level={3} id="install-cli">In a shell</Heading>
      <pre><code>{`npm install -g @tabnas/mcp    # installs the tabnas command`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        <code>tabnas parse | validate | diagnose | test | plugins | compare</code>,
        each mirroring one MCP tool. The skills teach the CLI spellings, so
        they work with or without a server connected.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <Heading level={2} id="install">3 · Install the engine</Heading>
      <pre><code>{`npm install @tabnas/parser @tabnas/abnf`}</code></pre>
      <pre><code>{`go get github.com/tabnas/parser/go`}</code></pre>
      <p class="note">
        Pin exact versions. Everything is pre-1.0, so a caret range will refuse
        the next minor release without telling you.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <Heading level={2} id="do-not-start-from-scratch">4 · Do not start from scratch</Heading>
      <p>
        This is the step most worth spending time on. Extension is the cheapest
        path and the one the engine is designed for: if something close to your
        format already parses, start there and add rules. JSONC is JSON plus
        comments; jsonic is JSONC with the quoting relaxed. Check the
        <a href="/releases">package list</a> before writing anything — or ask
        the <code>list_plugins</code> MCP tool (<code>tabnas plugins</code>),
        which answers from each plugin's own descriptor.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Parsing a JSON dialect? Start from <a href={REPO("jsonic")} target="_blank" rel="noopener">jsonic</a> and remove or add rules.</li>
        <li>Need operators and precedence? Add <a href={REPO("expr")} target="_blank" rel="noopener">expr</a> rather than hand-rolling a Pratt parser.</li>
        <li>Need <code>@name</code>-style forms? Add <a href={REPO("directive")} target="_blank" rel="noopener">directive</a>.</li>
        <li>Need free text between delimiters? Add <a href={REPO("hoover")} target="_blank" rel="noopener">hoover</a>.</li>
        <li>Composing several documents into one parse? Add <a href={REPO("multisource")} target="_blank" rel="noopener">multisource</a>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        <a href="/examples">aontu</a> is the worked example: a whole
        configuration language assembled from five of these plugins, defining no
        parser of its own.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <Heading level={2} id="the-rule-table">5 · The rule table</Heading>
      <p>
        This is the form to emit. It is plain data, so you can validate it,
        diff it, and print it before anything runs.
      </p>
      <CodeTabs id="agents-rule-table" label="A rule table, loaded and exercised" />

      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Field</th><th>On an alternate, means</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>s</code></th><td>Match this token sequence — one token, or several for lookahead.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>p</code></th><td><strong>Push</strong> a child rule — it becomes a child node.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>r</code></th><td><strong>Repeat</strong> a rule at the same stack depth — no nesting, same parent.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>a</code></th><td>Action: a function, a <code>@ref</code> name, a <code>$</code>-builtin, or an array of them.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>c</code></th><td>Condition — the alternate only applies when it holds.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>{"{}"}</code></th><td>The empty alternate. Ends the phase. Without one, no match is a parse error.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        <code>p</code> versus <code>r</code> is the distinction that catches
        people out. Push nests, so the child's <code>parent</code> is the pushing
        rule. Repeat stays at the same depth, so every repetition shares one
        parent — which is what makes an accumulator a single value in a single
        place.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <Heading level={2} id="actions-without-code">6 · Actions, ideally without code</Heading>
      <p>
        A grammar that only recognises input returns nothing. Actions build the
        result. There are three ways to attach them, and you should prefer them
        in this order:
      </p>

      <Heading level={3} id="builtin-actions">Builtin actions — no functions at all</Heading>
      <p>
        The engine ships <code>$</code>-suffixed builtins, merged into the ref
        map when the grammar loads. Referenced by name, they let you emit a
        grammar that is <strong>pure JSON with no code in it</strong> — which is
        the safest thing you can hand to someone else, and the only kind of
        grammar the MCP tools will accept.
      </p>
      <CodeTabs id="agents-builtin-actions" label="A grammar with no code in it" />
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Builtin</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>@object$</code></th><td><code>r.node = {"{}"}</code></td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>@array$</code></th><td><code>r.node = []</code></td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>@key$</code></th><td>Capture the matched key token.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>@setval$</code></th><td>Assign the child's node as an object property.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>@push$</code></th><td>Append the child's node to an array.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>@value$</code></th><td>Resolve the matched scalar token.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>@reset$</code></th><td>Clear the parent-seeded node.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>@node$</code> <code>@capture$</code> <code>@bubble$</code></th><td>Rebuild the <code>{"{ rule, src, kids }"}</code> tree. Used by the ABNF compiler.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <Heading level={3} id="named-refs">Named refs — code, bound out of band</Heading>
      <p>
        Keeps the grammar declarative while the code lives in the host
        program. Two kinds of name: alternate marks (open- or close-phase),
        and rule-phase hooks (<code>bo</code>, <code>ao</code>,
        <code>bc</code>, <code>ac</code> — before/after open and close).
        Keep results on the node, not in a variable outside the parse, so
        the grammar stays reusable.
      </p>
      <CodeTabs id="agents-named-refs" label="A rule-phase hook and an alternate mark" />
      <p class="note">
        Mark names are assigned by the compiler from each alternate's leading
        discriminator. <strong>Do not guess them.</strong> Ask:
        <code>tabnas-abnf --marks -f grammar.abnf</code>.
      </p>

      <Heading level={3} id="inline-functions">Inline functions</Heading>
      <p>
        Written straight onto the alternate as <code>a</code>. The most direct,
        but the grammar is now code and can no longer be serialised or safely
        shared.
      </p>
      <CodeTabs id="agents-inline-actions" label="Actions written onto the alternates" />
      <p>
        <code>r</code> is the rule instance, <code>r.node</code> the value it
        carries, <code>r.o</code> the tokens matched in the open phase (so
        <code>r.o[0].val</code> is the first token's value, already a number),
        and <code>r.parent</code> / <code>r.child</code> the neighbouring rules.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <Heading level={2} id="constraints">7 · Constraints to design around</Heading>
      <p>These are the things that will bite. All of them are by design.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Deterministic, no backtracking.</strong> Alternates are
          tried in order and the first match wins. Two alternates that can't be
          told apart from their leading tokens resolve to whichever comes
          first — order them deliberately.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Ambiguity is not supported.</strong> One parse or an error.
          If you need every valid parse, this is the wrong engine.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>You write the lexer.</strong> tabnas separates lexing from
          parsing and is about the parsing half. Built-ins like <code>NR</code>
          and fixed tokens cover a lot; beyond that, you assemble it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Left recursion is rewritten, not supported.</strong> The ABNF
          compiler applies <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Left_recursion#Removing_all_left_recursion" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Paull's
          algorithm</a>, so <code>P = P a / b</code>
          becomes <code>P = b *(a)</code>. The tree comes out flat rather than
          left-nested, and a purely left-recursive rule is an error.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A tail self-reference compiles to a repeat.</strong>
          <code>X = prefix [ sep X ]</code> becomes a same-depth
          <code>r:</code> repeat, so <code>r.parent</code> is the wrapping
          rule for every repetition and <code>r.parent.node</code> is the
          accumulator idiom — identical to a hand-written table. Other sugar
          (<code>( … )</code>, <code>*( … )</code>) still desugars into
          generated group rules, where <code>parent</code> may be synthetic;
          check with <code>tabnas-abnf --marks</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Empty alternates matter.</strong> A phase with no matching
          alternate is a parse error. <code>{"{}"}</code> is how a rule ends.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <Heading level={2} id="verify">8 · Verify your work</Heading>
      <p>
        A grammar that looks right and parses one happy-path input is not
        evidence. The MCP tools and the CLI from
        <a href="#skills-and-mcp">section 2</a> exist for exactly this loop.
        Before reporting success:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Validate before running.</strong>
          <code>tabnas validate --grammar g.json</code> (the
          <code>validate_grammar</code> tool) checks a serialized grammar
          against the engine's JSON Schema and loads it in a fresh instance —
          so a malformed table fails as a named error, not a misparse.
        </li>
        <li>Parse several known-good inputs and assert the <em>values</em>, not just that it didn't throw.</li>
        <li>Parse known-bad inputs and confirm they're rejected. A grammar that accepts everything is a common failure.</li>
        <li>
          <strong>Read the diagnostic, not the message.</strong> On a failure,
          <code>tabnas diagnose</code> (<code>explain_parse_error</code>)
          returns code, position, expected tokens and the rule stack; the
          <code>code</code> is a URL at <a href="/errors">/errors</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Pin behaviour with fixtures.</strong> Write
          <code>.tsv</code> rows — input, expected value or
          <code>ERROR:&lt;code&gt;</code> — and run them with
          <code>tabnas test --spec fixtures.tsv</code>
          (<code>test_grammar</code>). One file runs against both runtimes.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Changing a grammar others already use?</strong>
          <code>tabnas compare</code> (<code>compare_grammars</code>) reports
          what a change provably keeps, what it observably changes, and with
          what confidence — evidence, never a bare verdict.
        </li>
        <li>Run <code>tabnas-abnf --marks</code> if an action didn't fire.</li>
        <li>Use <a href={REPO("debug")} target="_blank" rel="noopener">@tabnas/debug</a> to describe the live grammar and render it back to ABNF — if the round-trip isn't what you meant, the grammar isn't either.</li>
        <li>Use <a href={REPO("railroad")} target="_blank" rel="noopener">@tabnas/railroad</a> to draw it when the structure is hard to hold in your head.</li>
        <li>Try it in the <a href="/playground">playground</a> — it takes ABNF or a rule table and shows both the tree and the value.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <Heading level={2} id="agents-md">9 · Per-package instructions</Heading>
      <p>
        Every package repository ships an <code>AGENTS.md</code> with its
        layout, conventions, test commands, and the non-obvious things to know
        before changing it. Read the one for the package you're touching — start
        with <a href={AGENTS_MD("parser")} target="_blank" rel="noopener">parser</a>.
      </p>
      {groups.map((g) => (
        <div class="agrp">
          <p class="agrp-head">{g.label}</p>
          <p class="chips">
            {g.items.map((p) => (
              <a class="chip" href={AGENTS_MD(p.name)} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{p.name}</a>
            ))}
          </p>
        </div>
      ))}
      <p class="note">
        Work lands in TypeScript first, then Go to match; both run the same
        fixtures, so a change that doesn't really work fails loudly. Agent-written
        contributions are welcome and need no disclosure — see
        <a href="/community#this-project-is-ai-friendly">community</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <Heading level={2} id="this-site">10 · This site, machine-readable</Heading>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong><a href="/llms.txt">llms.txt</a></strong> indexes the site
          and <strong><a href="/llms-full.txt">llms-full.txt</a></strong> is
          the full documentation text. Both are generated from the site's own
          pages, so they cannot fall behind them.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong><a href="/versions.json">versions.json</a></strong> says
          which package versions this documentation describes — worth reading
          before trusting an example against whatever you have installed.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong><a href="/errors">/errors</a></strong> is every error code
          the engine and its plugins can raise, one page per code at
          <code>/errors/&lt;code&gt;</code>. A diagnostic's <code>code</code> is
          therefore a URL you can follow.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Source for everything is under
        <a href={GITHUB_ORG} target="_blank" rel="noopener">github.com/tabnas</a>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .ag { max-width: 54rem; }
  .ag :global(h1) { font-size: clamp(2.1rem, 5vw, 3rem); margin: 0 0 1rem; }
  .lede { font-size: 1.15rem; color: var(--ink-soft); max-width: 64ch; }

  section { margin-top: 3rem; }
  .ag :global(h2) { font-size: 1.6rem; margin: 0 0 0.85rem; }
  .ag :global(h3) { font-size: 1.1rem; margin: 1.75rem 0 0.5rem; color: var(--accent); }
  section p { color: var(--ink-soft); max-width: 66ch; }
  .note { font-size: 0.92rem; }

  pre {
    margin: 0.75rem 0; padding: 0.9rem 1.1rem; overflow-x: auto;
    background: var(--surface); border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 10px;
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.85rem; line-height: 1.65;
  }
  pre > code { color: var(--ink); }

  ul { color: var(--ink-soft); padding-left: 1.2rem; max-width: 66ch; }
  li { margin-bottom: 0.6rem; }
  li strong { color: var(--ink); }

  .table-wrap { overflow-x: auto; margin: 1rem 0 1.25rem; }
  table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 0.9rem; }
  th, td { text-align: left; padding: 0.5rem 0.8rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); vertical-align: top; }
  thead th {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.66rem; text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em; color: var(--ink-mute); font-weight: 400;
  }
  tbody th { font-weight: 400; white-space: nowrap; }
  tbody td { color: var(--ink-soft); }

  .agrp { margin-top: 1rem; }
  .agrp-head {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.68rem; text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em; color: var(--ink-mute); margin: 0 0 0.4rem;
  }
  .chips { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.4rem; margin: 0 0 1rem; }
  .chip {
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.8rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft); text-decoration: none;
    border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: 999px; padding: 0.2rem 0.65rem;
  }
  .chip:hover { color: var(--primary); border-color: var(--border-strong); }
</style>
